# Scoring a lead

Every lead that flows through the [[Pipeline]] eventually needs one number: how
much should we care about it? That number is the `score` field on a `%Lead{}`,
and the unit on this page is the thing that fills it. It is a small, pure,
deterministic function — the kind of code that *wants* a test — so this file is
also where we teach **placement** (where code runs) and the **weave gate's
test** (a red test ships no bundle).

## Placement: client vs server

A unit declares **where it runs**, and there are exactly two answers.

- **client** — the browser. The unit compiles to wasm and runs there, untrusted
  and restricted. Elixir authored for the client runs on an Elixir-in-wasm
  runtime; the [[Chart]] island is a client unit.
- **server** — your backend, the nexus. Native Elixir on the BEAM, real OTP, not
  wasm. It is the trusted tier: it can be granted secrets, the database, the
  network. The [[Enrich]] unit lives here because it needs `net`.

`:score` is declared **server**. Not because it touches anything privileged — it
is a pure arithmetic function with no grants at all — but because it is part of
the trusted compute lane that the [[Pipeline]] runs as it folds a batch of leads.
Placement is a property of the unit, *not* a sandbox: a `sandbox` is an isolation
boundary you join, never a place you run. Keep the two ideas apart.

## The function

The contract is `score(%Lead{}) -> integer`, on a 0–100 scale. The shape is two
contributions added together and rounded: revenue points (the dominant signal)
plus size points (employee headcount). Both are clamped so a single enormous lead
can't blow past the ceiling, and the whole thing is capped at 100.

server :score do
  @revenue_ceiling 100_000
  @size_ceiling 60

  # Revenue is worth up to 70 of the 100 points; headcount the remaining 30.
  # Both are clamped before weighting so one outlier can't dominate the scale.
  def score(%Lead{revenue: revenue, employees: employees}) do
    revenue_points = weigh(revenue, @revenue_ceiling, 70)
    size_points = weigh(employees, @size_ceiling, 30)

    (revenue_points + size_points)
    |> Float.round()
    |> trunc()
    |> min(100)
  end

  # Map a raw value onto a 0..weight band, clamping the input to [0, ceiling]
  # first so the fraction never exceeds 1.0.
  defp weigh(value, ceiling, weight) do
    fraction =
      value
      |> max(0)
      |> min(ceiling)
      |> Kernel./(ceiling)

    fraction * weight
  end
end

A few things this code is quietly teaching. The `%Lead{}` pattern in the head is
the **type** — there is no separate schema, the `defstruct` from the canonical
`Lead` is the contract, and matching on its fields is how we name them. The
private `weigh/3` helper is *not* part of the unit's interface: only public
functions become wasm **exports**, so `score/1` is what the [[Pipeline]] and the
[[Dashboard]] can call, and `weigh/3` stays internal. And there is not a single
grant in sight — a pure function imports nothing, so the Dock fulfills nothing.

## The test — and why it gates the build

Because `:score` is pure, it is trivially testable, and the format treats a test
as a first-class unit that the **weave gate runs before it emits a bundle**. If
any test goes red, `work bundle` produces *no* `salesapp.html`. That is the whole
safety contract: you cannot ship a sales app whose scoring math is broken.

We assert against the three canonical sample leads. Acme and Initech both land in
the `:scored` band; Globex is a weaker, still-`:enriched` lead and should score
lower than either.

test :score do
  alias Lead

  @acme    %Lead{name: "Acme",    domain: "acme.co",    revenue: 50_000, employees: 20, score: 70, status: :scored}
  @globex  %Lead{name: "Globex",  domain: "globex.io",  revenue: 12_000, employees: 8,  score: 41, status: :enriched}
  @initech %Lead{name: "Initech", domain: "initech.com", revenue: 90_000, employees: 55, score: 88, status: :scored}

  test "scores stay on the 0..100 scale" do
    for lead <- [@acme, @globex, @initech] do
      points = score(lead)
      assert points >= 0
      assert points <= 100
    end
  end

  test "a strong lead outranks a weak one" do
    assert score(@initech) > score(@acme)
    assert score(@acme) > score(@globex)
  end

  test "an empty lead scores zero" do
    assert score(%Lead{}) == 0
  end

  test "revenue and headcount are both clamped at the ceiling" do
    whale = %Lead{revenue: 10_000_000, employees: 5_000}
    assert score(whale) == 100
  end
end

The first test pins the **range invariant** — no matter how extreme the input, a
score is a number between 0 and 100. The second pins the **ordering** we actually
sell on: Initech (high revenue, big team) beats Acme beats Globex, which is the
ranking the [[Dashboard]] charts. The empty-`%Lead{}` case proves the floor, and
the `whale` case proves the clamps in `weigh/3` really hold the ceiling.

## Where this fits

`:score` is one stage of the [[Pipeline]]: a batch is seeded, run through
[[Enrich]] to fill `revenue`/`employees`, then mapped through `score/1` to set the
`score` field and promote each lead's `status` to `:scored`. From there the
[[Chart]] island visualizes the distribution and the agents in the
[[The Analyst Agent]] flow decide who is worth a draft. One pure function, one test, and a
gate that refuses to ship if the math drifts.
